<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n24370" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">δημήγορ-ος</span></b> , ὁ, (<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἀγορεύω</span>) <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b><i>popular orator,</i> mostly in a bad sense, <i><span class="en">
				Pl.
				<u>Grg.</u>
				520b
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				<u>Lg.</u>
				908d
			</span></i>, etc.; 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὅρκος ἑτᾳίρας ταὐτὸ καὶ δημηγόρου</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Diph.
					101
				</span></i>
			; but <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">δ. ἀγαθοί,</span> opp.
				<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ῥήτορες φαῦλοι,</span>
			<i><span class="en">
				X.
				<u>Mem.</u>
				2.6.15
			</span></i>: as Adj., <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">δημηγόρος,
					<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ον</span>, τιμαὶ δ.</span>
			<i>a speaker's</i> honours, <i><span class="en">
				E.
				<u>Hec.</u>
				254
			</span></i>; <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">στροφαὶ δημηγόροι</span>
			<i>rhetorical</i> tricks, <i><span class="en">
				A.
				<u>Supp.</u>
				623
			</span></i>.</div>
